    gone forever — and for due dates specifically, "you weren't looking" is the normal case, which makes this the
    weakest fit.
  - [1mBell icon + notification centre[0m — a badge with an unread count, a panel listing what fired, mark-read and dismiss.
    Notifications become durable records with state. Costs you a small store and its persistence, and the reload
    semantics need deciding (see below).
  - [1mBrowser [38;5;153mNotification[39m API[0m — real OS-level notifications that work when the tab is backgrounded. The genuine version
    of this feature, but it needs a permission prompt, degrades to nothing when denied, and still needs an in-page
    fallback — so it's additive to one of the above, not an alternative.

  Cheap to change later: adding the OS channel on top, if the delivery seam is a channel interface. Expensive to change
  later: retrofitting unread/dismiss state onto a toast-only design — that's the store, the persistence, and the
  task-to-notification relationship all at once.

  [1mMy recommendation: bell + centre, with toasts as a second channel on the same delivery interface, and the OS channel [0m
  [1mleft out of the prototype.[0m The centre is what makes a due-date system usable, the toast is ~20 lines once the bus
  exists, and skipping the permission prompt keeps the prototype free of a flow that's annoying to test.

  One thing I'll fold into the design rather than ask separately: notifications persist in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m alongside tasks,
  and on load the scheduler reconciles — anything that came due while you were away is already in the centre, marked
  unread. That's the behaviour that makes closing the tab safe.
